This will output the first eigenvalue, the first eigenvector, and the result of the multiplication, showing the relationship between the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a matrix.  Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors A key question in linear algebra is "What are the eigenvectors and eigenvalues of a matrix?". The eigenvector of a matrix is a vector that, when that matrix is multiplied by it, only scales the vector, and does not change its direction. The eigenvalue is a scalar value that indicates how much the eigenvector changes when the linear transformation represented by the matrix is applied to it. Eigenvectors and eigenvalues are very important as they form the bases of many important mathematical models and analytical techniques, including the famous Principal Component Analysis.  Properties of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors: 1. Any scalar multiple of an eigenvector remains an eigenvector. The corresponding eigenvalue is the square of that scalar. 2. The dot product of an eigenvector with itself is 1. 3. Every matrix has at least one eigenvector and one eigenvalue. 4. The product of a matrix and its eigenvectors is again an eigenvector.  5. A matrix's eigenvalues and eigenvectors are unique up to a scalar multiple.
