Here we have solved the system of linear equations with numpy's built-in function for finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors. The `matrix.T` operation transposes our matrix and `matrix.I` computes the multiplicative inverse of the matrix. Then we multiply the `eigenvectors` by the element wise squaring of `eigenvalues` to get the desired results.  Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors and the Determinant and Trace In Linear algebra, the trace of a square matrix is the sum of the eigenvalues of the matrix. The determinant is a scalar value that determines the "volume" of the parallelipiped spanned by the columns of the matrix. Given a matrix A, the determinant of A is denoted as det(A). The determinant of a 1x1 matrix is the unique entry, the determinant of a 2x2 matrix is the computed value, and the determinant of larger matrices is computed using the principle of minors and cofactors (determinant of matrix obtained by removing a row and column from the original matrix, and multiplying each cofactor by -1 to the power of the position of the cofactor in the row and column, and then adding those cofactors together). The trace of a matrix is defined as the sum of the main diagonal elements (top left to bottom right) of the matrix. The trace of a 1x1 matrix is the single element in the matrix, the trace of a 2x2 matrix is the sum of its diagonal elements, and the trace of a larger matrix is computed by adding up the diagonal elements.
